INTERPRETATION CHANGES

The grocery-surplus leak funds the authenticity economy Soites can't enter
Soites compresses the at-home basket, freeing household income that reallocates to eating out. That surplus lands in the independent restaurant sector and stays there: restaurants compete on authenticity and experience, not price, so Soites' entire advantage set (scale, private label, waste-matching, data) does not convert — its costs there are wage-and-equipment-bound like anyone's, and its brand is a net negative to the exact holdout segment (principle/privacy/exclusivity/experience) that drives discretionary dining. Soites therefore avoids the restaurant business on purpose, operating only convenience-tier outlets in the FFTZ and otherwise acting as landlord to independent restaurants inside the zone. Consequence: the accused destroyer of local retail is the involuntary patron of the local authenticity economy, and unlike the grocery surplus it cannot recapture the spend. The Coalition critique and its inversion both hold, and the inversion is now un-hedged — there is no loop-back.

The processed-food floor is subsidized by the meat-and-dairy coproduct web
The cheapness of Susia's processed basket is not only LV grain compression. The bison-meat and cattle-dairy systems throw off cheap coproducts — tallow, butterfat, hide/leather, bone, offal — as byproducts of production already happening for meat and milk. These underwrite downstream goods (cookie fat, leather, soap, gelatin). Explains why processed goods (cookies especially) come out cheap without changing any upstream number. Affects future food-economy and LV-agricultural articles.

Two axes of geographic price, converging for opposite reasons
Price varies on two independent axes. Logistics axis: distance from the Nayotai rail spine (FFTZ cheapest → national baseline → rail-periphery like Nessel dearest); this is a transport-cost effect on the good, and it is *why a national baseline price is a meaningful quantity at all* — dense rail collapses regional spread. Cost-of-living axis: urban land/wage overhead at the point of sale (Neoveli/Duolij cores dearest), a retail-overhead effect on the *store*, not the good — so its size scales with a good's overhead share (modest on commodity staples, steep on prepared/fresh). The two axes can produce the same shelf price for inverse reasons: rail-periphery bread is dear because it travelled far to a thin market; urban-core bread is dear because it sits on expensive land in a thick one.

The FEZ discount and the Sisra discount are the same lever: overhead share
Both the FFTZ retail discount and the Sisra private-label discount scale with how much of a good's price is strippable overhead versus irreducible commodity/labor input. This unifies three separate findings: Sisra barely beats commodity staples but guts processed goods; the FFTZ discount is deepest on packaged goods and shallowest on fresh/labor; and the Rǎzuraz grocery run rewards processed-basket households far more than raw-staple households. One mechanism (overhead share) predicts all three, and it sorts *who saves how much* by *what they buy*.

Fusion is a post-snapshot pressure, not a present one
The 2080 basket is priced on thorium-era energy (commercial 2051), which is already inside the low baselines. Commercial fusion is 2081 — just arriving, not yet underwriting anything. A future 2090s basket is a different document: fusion cheapens energy-intensive links (refrigeration, cold-chain, greenhouse, frozen goods) a further step. `%% TODO: 2090s basket reprice under fusion %%`

